
# Project 01 - Secure Multi-Container Deployment

Campus Helpdesk System

## Project Overview

This project explores secure multi-container deployment using Docker Compose, Apache HTTPD and PostgreSQL within a simulated campus helpdesk environment.

The project focuses on infrastructure configuration, network segmentation, resource management, container hardening, persistent storage and database disaster recovery.

## Technical Objectives

- Deploy Apache HTTPD and PostgreSQL containers.
- Configure CPU and memory restrictions.
- Implement frontend and backend network segmentation.
- Restrict direct database exposure.
- Apply least-privilege security controls.
- Maintain persistent database storage.
- Perform database backup and restoration.

## Service Configuration

| Parameter | Web Service | Database Service |
|---|---|---|
| Service | helpdesk | database |
| Container | helpdesk-web | helpdesk-db |
| Image | httpd:2.4 | postgres:16 |
| Published Port | 8105:80 | Disabled |
| CPU Limit | 0.6 | 0.8 |
| Memory Limit | 320 MiB | 640 MiB |
| Restart Policy | unless-stopped | always |

## Network Architecture

| Network | Driver | Connected Services |
|---|---|---|
| helpdesk-front | bridge | Web |
| helpdesk-back | bridge | Web and Database |

The database is restricted to the backend network without a published host port.

## Docker Compose Configuration

```yaml
services:
  helpdesk:
    image: httpd:2.4
    container_name: helpdesk-web
    ports:
      - "8105:80"
    cpus: 0.6
    mem_limit: 320m
    restart: unless-stopped
    networks:
      - helpdesk-front
      - helpdesk-back
    privileged: false
    cap_drop:
      - ALL
    security_opt:
      - no-new-privileges:true

  database:
    image: postgres:16
    container_name: helpdesk-db
    environment:
      POSTGRES_DB: ticketsdb
      POSTGRES_USER: supportuser
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:?Set password}
    cpus: 0.8
    mem_limit: 640m
    restart: always
    networks:
      - helpdesk-back
    volumes:
      - helpdesk-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data

networks:
  helpdesk-front:
    name: helpdesk-front
    driver: bridge

  helpdesk-back:
    name: helpdesk-back
    driver: bridge

volumes:
  helpdesk-data:
    name: helpdesk-data
```

## Security Controls

| Control | Purpose |
|---|---|
| privileged: false | Avoid privileged execution |
| cap_drop: ALL | Remove Linux capabilities |
| no-new-privileges | Prevent additional privilege acquisition |
| Network segmentation | Restrict unnecessary communication |
| Resource limits | Control resource consumption |
| Database port isolation | Reduce direct database exposure |

## Deployment and Verification

```powershell
docker compose config --quiet
docker compose up -d
docker compose ps
docker stats --no-stream
docker network inspect helpdesk-front
docker network inspect helpdesk-back
docker logs helpdesk-web
docker exec helpdesk-db pg_isready -U supportuser -d ticketsdb
```

Web service testing is performed using localhost on port 8105.

## Persistent Storage and Recovery

| Parameter | Configuration |
|---|---|
| Database | ticketsdb |
| Test Table | tickets |
| Original Volume | helpdesk-data |
| Backup Directory | C:\HelpdeskBackup |
| Backup Archive | helpdesk-data.tar.gz |
| Restored Volume | helpdesk-restored |

The recovery procedure involves stopping PostgreSQL, creating a physical volume backup, restoring the archive into a separate volume and verifying the original database records.

The final Compose configuration uses helpdesk-restored as an external volume after restoration.

